// Three arguments, as both published calls use it: Figure 7
// (atan 2.25 (dissolve 0.1 0.11 time) 0.15) and Figure 12
// (atan (log ...) #(0.17 0.08 0.75) 0.37). Neither paper says what the
// arguments do, so this follows Figure 12: the angle is
// atan2(v1 * scale, v0), returned in half-turns (divided by pi, so
// -1...1) to match sin and cos. Figure 12's first argument is a log that
// changes sign at y = 0; with it on the x side, the outer cos gives a light
// top and a dark bottom, as in Sims' image.
node "atan"(v0, v1, scale: scalar) {
	return atan2(v1 * scale, v0) / 3.14159265
}
